  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nI propose a simulated annealing (SA) algorithm with carefully designed feasible moves to minimize the maximum overlap integral. The key innovation is using a combination of structural changes and fine-grained adjustments to escape local optima. Unlike local search methods, SA allows occasional uphill moves to explore new regions, making it suitable for non-convex optimization problems like this. The approach balances exploration and exploitation through adaptive cooling and move selection probabilities.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nFor piecewise constant h(x) over [0,2] with n=250 samples:\n- dx = 2.0/n\n- Objective: minimize C5 = max_k [np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx]\n- Feasible function space: h[x] \u2208 [0,1] with sum(h)*dx = 1\n- Primary constraints enforced by projection\n- Special consideration: overlaps are computed over all n+249 lags due to mode='full'\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization\n- Start from provided parent or random feasible solution (n=250)\n- Project solution to ensure feasibility: \n    dx = 2.0/n\n    target_sum = n/2\n    Clip values to [0,1] and adjust sum if needed\n\nb. Core SA algorithm:\nLifetime = budget_s * 0.9 * (2.0/n)  # Grace period\nT0 = 10.0  # Initial temperature\nalpha = 0.995  # Temperature decay rate\np_global = 0.2  # Probability of global moves\np_local = 0.8  # Probability of local moves\n\nFor each iteration:\n    Prioritize move types:\n    - Global move (20%): Swap segments to break self-overlap patterns\n        Choose random segment length L (20 to n//5)\n        Find pattern of length L that creates significant overlap\n        Cut and paste it somewhere else in the function\n    - Local move (80%): Local descent near significant overlaps\n        Identify top-k lags with largest overlaps\n        For each lag: search spatial window around discontinuity for small improvements\n        Use gradient information to safely update function values\n\n    Evaluate new state (check bounds and integral)\n    Calculate score difference \u0394C5 (toward minimization)\n    Apply Metropolis rule: accept worse states with probability exp(-\u0394C5/T)\n\n    Adjust temperature: T = T0 * alpha^(iterations/cooling_rate)\n\nNote: The move types are designed to handle both global structure (large discontinuities) and local alignment issues in the overlap.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nStandard SA with:\n- Variable-length cuts (20-50 samples) for global swaps\n- Auto-tuned descent window (starts wide and narrows)\n- Dynamic temperature adjustment based on acceptance rate:\n    Acceptance rate < 0.2: Too high temperature\n    Acceptance rate > 0.8: Too low temperature\n\nFallback protections:\n- If cooling completes without improvement, try restarting from best-so-far\n- Include validation for NaN scores and integral enforcement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nMain cost: O(10^6) calls to correlation (n=250) which is O(625e6) operations, balanced against 1100s budget (project feasible, score computations, and move applications)\n\nTerminal conditions:\n- Timed out (return best found)\n- Temperature below T_min (0.1)\n- Improvement stall criteria\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nMust satisfy:\n- Full pass through validation function:\n    - Check feasibility: sum(h)*dx \u2248 1, 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n    - Compute exact C5 using reference method:\n        c5_val = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx\n- Optimal window: record best value for return\n- Fallback: if all else fails, return parent-proven solution",
